  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Number of new acceptable substitutes identified for hazardous chemicals and carbon intensive and high resource consuming technologies in specific health care products by the project</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2018">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">Baseline 0</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Development of the Index
2. Development of Policies/strategies and guidance for the health sector
3. Support the identification of Health sector commodities for substitution
4. Global Manufacturers forum
5. Capacity Building of Manufacturers and procurement officers
6. Finalize the development of SPHS platform</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Developed the beta version of the index which highlights key focus areas that include resource utilisation, chemical toxicity, greenhouse gases, and the promotion of social dimensions (human rights, gender and labour rights).
2.Development of policies and strategies in project countries. In Vietnam a policy on plastic elimination was developed and approved. In Moldova, the project worked with the government and stakeholders to produce a &#x201c;Policy Analysis on the Use of Potentially Harmful Substances&#x201d; and the application of sustainable procurement practices for the health sector. In India, SHiPP supported the National Centre for Disease Control (NCDC) to adopt the organization&#x2019;s phase-out of its PVC gloves policy
3. A Criteria for interventions to create less toxic, carbon intensive and high resource consuming technologies identified and made available to Project Countries. 
4. Organized the 2020 Global Manufacturers forum in Tanzania with about 500 delegates attending the event. This resulted in key policy dialogues involving buyers and manufacturers of health commodities. Consensus was reached on the definition of sustainability and how the index being developed should look like. 
5. UNDP, HCWH and partners held a total of 14 webinars in 2020 with over 1,000 participants from 50 countries and included representatives from governments, development partners, health care organizations, academia and Civil Society Organisations.
6. The SPHS platform was fully developed, delivered and launched. It is the main microsite for the SHiPP project</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Finalize and launch the Sustainable Procurement index for Health
2. development of at least 10 policies and strategies in project countries and other scale up regions in Latin America and South East Asia
3.Provide coordination and secretarial services for Joint SHiPP/SPHS/One Planet Platforms.
4. Conduct capacity building for sustainable procurement for the health systems and ministries on adaptation and implementation of less - toxic, carbon intensive and high resource consuming technologies
5. Hold the 4th Global Manufacturers Forum in Saudi Arabia on the margins of the G20 summit
6. Conduct the Terminal Evaluation for the SHiPP Project</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. The sustainable Procurement Index was launched in November 2021 and is already supporting procurement processes. UNOPS, the lead procurement UN agency, has referenced the Index within its newly developed criteria list for mandatory sustainability requirements for specific categories of goods and services. This forms part of their Sustainable Procurement Framework which has recently been revised to include medicines and medical devices. This will facilitate the wider exposure and application of the index dimensions considering the wide volume of health care procurement that the agency manages&#x2019;. In Brazil, SHiPP partner Projecto Hospitais has developed a Sustainable Procurement challenge which will incorporate the index and form the basis for implementing the SPIH in Brazil.
2. Over 10 policies, strategies and tools have been developed. In Tanzania, SHiPP supported the Government to finalise the Health Care Waste Management (HCWM) Guidelines and Standards and facilitated the incorporation of sustainable HCWM treatment methods and procedures into national policies. In Southeast Asia, the SHiPP team conducted national health policy desk reviews on sustainable procurement in Indonesia and the Philippines. This activity is contributing to changing practices at the different levels of the health care systems of these countries. 
3. In order to reinvigorate the global community&#x2019;s interest in the One Planet SPP Interest Group on Health, UNDP and HCWH developed an online feedback form to facilitate the participation of current and prospective members to share their vision of how to jointly move forward in 2021. The form also allows for feedback on the key added value of the multi-stakeholder Interest Group, and how members plan to contribute to its roll-out in 2021. 
4. A number of Capacity Building efforts were done in SHiPP countries and beyond. In Brazil, three symposia were held with the managers of the S&#xe3;o Paulo State Health Network, which led to the establishment of a working group to solve over the next four years challenges related to sustainable procurement, climate, energy and waste management in 70 percent of S&#xe3;o Paulo State &#x2019;s health units. In Viet Nam, training materials were developed based on guidelines and tools provided by SHiPP to prepare for a large national training on sustainable production and procurement. These materials will be incorporated in the curriculum to support the country&#x2019;s service trainings
5. The 2021 Global manufacturers forum was held in Cali Colombia from 17 to 18th November. The Minister of Health for Colombia committed his country to a number of actions considering the number of climate related incidents and highlighted that Climate change is a health emergency that requires urgent measures to address the negative effect being experienced across the world. The decarbonization session recommended the inclusion of health in NDCs. This will support countries to adopt concrete measures to cut emissions (which are equivalent to 4.4% of global net emissions (2 gigatons of carbon dioxide equivalent) from the health sector. 
6. The Terminal Evaluation was conducted by an independent consultant. The report highlighted key achievements including a number of policies, tools and strategies.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
